1515719.steps:
1.Login to the Firewall from default LAN interface;
2.Navigate to Network&gt;Interface page, make sure the interface table has 8 columns as following: - Name - Zone - IP Address - Subnet Mask - IP Assignment - Status - Comment - Configure 
3.For those columns, make sure the interface X0 has default settings as "X0, LAN, 192.168.168.168, 255.255.255.0, static, (depending on the auto-negotiation result between PC and FW), Default LAN";
4.Make sure the interface X1 has default settings as "X1, WAN, 0.0.0.0, 0.0.0.0, N/A, No link, Default WAN"; 
5). As for other interfaces Xn, the values are "Xn, Unassigned, 0.0.0.0, 0.0.0.0, N/A, No link, (No comment)";

1515727.steps:
1).Login to the FW as admin from the default LAN interface;
2).Navigate to Network&gt;Interfaces, open the configure dialog of the default LAN, select the transparent Mode from the IP assignment dropdown box, make sure the contents on the page change; 
3).Make sure there is a new dropdown box transparent Range, expand it and make sure there are some Address Objects and create new address object item; 
4).Make sure the IP Address and subnet Mask disappeared from the dialog; 
5).Go to the Advanced tab, make sure there are two new check-boxes enable Gratuitous ARP Forwarding Towards WAN and enable Gratuitous ARP Forwarding Towards WAN , verify they can be checked;

1515728.steps:
1).Login to the FW as admin from the default LAN interface; 
2).Open the configure dialog of the default LAN and choose the "Transparent Mode"; 
3).Expand the dropdown list of Transparent Range, and make sure there is an item called "Create new address object..."; 
4).Choose the "Create new address object", make sure there is a "Add Address Object" dialog popped up; 
5).Create a host address object, a range address object and a network address object, make sure the correct objects added in Address Objects.

